Linux Basics

1. Linux Types
Debian-based: (Ubuntu, Kali) → Uses .deb packages and the apt package manager.
RPM-based: (RedHat, CentOS, Fedora) → Uses .rpm packages and yum or dnf for package management.

2. Linux File System Structure
/ → Root directory (everything starts here)
/home → User folders
/etc → Configuration files
/var → Logs
/usr → Installed apps and binaries
/bin, /sbin, /tmp, /opt → System essentials

3. Basic File and Folder Commands
mkdir foldername → Create a directory
cp source destination → Copy files/folders
mv source destination → Move or rename files/folders

4. Vim Editor Basics
Open a file: vim filename
Enter edit mode: Press i
Save and exit: Esc, type :wq, press Enter
Exit without saving: Esc, type :q!

5. Different Types of Files
Regular files (text, images)
Directories (folders)
Symbolic links (shortcuts)
Device files (represent hardware)
Sockets (process communication)

6. Searching Inside Files with grep
grep "pattern" filename

7. Viewing Files: less, more, head, tail
less filename → Scroll inside the file
more filename → View page by page
head filename → View the first 10 lines
tail filename → View the last 10 lines

8. Important Configuration Files
/etc/ssh/sshd_config → SSH settings
/etc/fstab → Disk mounting
/etc/hosts → Local DNS settings

9. Useful Commands: sed and awk
sed → Search and replace text inside files.
awk → A powerful text-processing tool for pattern scanning and processing.

10. User and Group Management
Add a user: sudo useradd username
Delete a user: sudo userdel username
Create a group: sudo groupadd groupname
Delete a group: sudo groupdel groupname

11. File Permissions Management
chmod — Change permission (read, write, execute)
chown — Change file owner/group

12. Sudo Command and Configuration
Give users admin privileges by adding them to the sudo group.
Always edit sudo permissions using sudo visudo safely.

13. Package Management
Debian-based: apt install packagename
RPM-based: yum install packagename or dnf install packagename

14. Archiving and Compression
Create a tar file: tar -cvf archive.tar folder/
Extract tar file: tar -xvf archive.tar
Create compressed archive: tar -czvf archive.tar.gz folder/

15. Cronjobs (Task Scheduling)
Edit scheduled tasks: crontab -e
Example: 0 2 * * * /home/user/backup.sh → This command will run the backup script every day at 2 AM automatically.
